  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Podosphaera minor is a fungal pathogen belonging to the powdery mildew family that affects various plants in New England regions. It creates characteristic white, powdery coatings on plant leaves and stems, reducing photosynthesis and plant vigor.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Distinctive white to grayish powder covering affected leaves, stems, and sometimes flowers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Chasmothecia Formation",
      "description": "Small dark fruiting bodies (chasmothecia) may appear on infected tissue as the season progresses",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Plants",
      "description": "Commonly infects hawthorn, apple, and related Rosaceae family plants in New England",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Podosphaera minor thrives in cool, dry conditions on ornamental and fruit-bearing plants in temperate New England gardens and forests.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many fungi, powdery mildews prefer dry conditions and can actually be inhibited by free water on leaves, making overhead watering a useful management strategy.",
